sadie2-d2-48k.anhrtf

Derived from the SADIE II Database (https://www.york.ac.uk/sadie-project/database.html, release v2-2,
https://zenodo.org/records/12092466), subject D2 (KEMAR), the 48 kHz 256-tap HRIRs
(D2_HRIR_SOFA/D2_48K_24bit_256tap_FIR_SOFA.sofa).

Copyright 2018, University of York. Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0 (LICENSE in this folder). All
measurements are Copyright University of York. Recorded and developed at The Audio Lab, Department of Electronic
Engineering, University of York, UK, by Cal Armstrong, Lewis Thresh and Gavin Kearney.

Reference: C. Armstrong, L. Thresh, D. Murphy, G. Kearney, "A Perceptual Evaluation of Individual and Non-Individual
HRTFs: A Case Study of the SADIE II Database", Applied Sciences 8(11), 2029 (2018), doi:10.3390/app8112029.

Modified by Anode (scripts/build-hrtf.py): a subset of the directions (a 2-degree azimuth grid at elevations -75 to 75
in steps of 15, and the two poles: 1982 of 8802), quantised to 16-bit integers with one scale, with each ear's delay
(the peak of the response low-passed at 1.5 kHz) added, in Anode's .anhrtf format.
